# duty-cycle-tick — full version changelog
# Extracted verbatim from the skill's own frontmatter, 2026-09-22 (context-floor item 2, Phase A).
# Newest first, matching the order it was accreted in. One entry per line.

v1.40 (2026-09-25) — **Two new START-side steps, both from same-day real incidents.** Step 1d (Docs only): PM ruled, after the 09-24 omnibus lapse, that producing the prior day's omnibus (or verifying it exists) plus nudging any agent with a missing/unclosed session log is now a FIXED part of Docs's daily START, not displaceable by a heavy morning -- HOST-Step-1a shape (cheap, role-gated, once per START). Step 1e (all roles): after Lead filed #1892 (main sat red 8.5 hours across ~35 pushes from 7 seats, nobody noticed -- the CI gate worked, the signal had nowhere to land), every START now checks main's latest gating-workflow (Code Quality / lint.yml) conclusion and prints it next to the heartbeat. Visibility only, not a gate. Tested the check before shipping it and caught a real bug in the first draft: `gh run list --branch main` silently returns a stale 12-day-old result instead of erroring on this repo -- dropped the flag and instead scan the last 10 runs for the first non-empty conclusion, since GitHub Actions frequently cancels a run when a newer push supersedes it mid-flight and a cancelled run must not be read as a failure.

v1.39 (2026-09-23) — **Rule 1's cron delete is now book-ended, not vigilance-dependent.** Lead's 09-22 STOP was missed because the drain-protection delete's re-arm half depended on remembering to act at an idle moment that never arrived -- correctly detected and backfilled by Step 0 the next morning, but the backstop firing is not the primary mechanism working. PM ratified the framing directly (in-conversation, relayed by Lead): "Rule 1 only works if it is book-ended by restoring a cron when finally ready to go idle" -- the delete and the restore are one obligation with two halves, not two independent steps. Mechanism adopted from Lead's proposal: when deleting the recurring cron to protect a drain, `CronCreate` a one-shot at that day's STOP slot in the same breath -- idempotent with a later normal re-arm, and a guaranteed backstop if the agent never returns to an explicit idle moment. Also recorded, not resolved: `CronCreate`'s own docs confirm jobs only fire while the REPL is idle (never mid-query), which means the interruption hazard Rule 1's delete exists to prevent may not be real on this runtime at all -- Lead named this as an "optional deeper question," and this edit deliberately ships only the conservative book-end fix, leaving whether to drop the delete entirely as a separate decision.

v1.38 (2026-09-22) — **Mail/task loop exit condition tightened to PM's exact two-consecutive-empty-rounds requirement, closing a real gap PM found by formalizing the flywheel as a 16-state table.** PM's rule: exit to idle ONLY on two consecutive (0 mail, 0 tasks) rounds; every other pattern -- including new mail/tasks in the round BEFORE the current one, even if the current round is itself clean -- continues the loop. The prior text said only "loop until nothing left, then exit," which a SINGLE clean pass through steps 1-2-2b-3 already satisfies -- weaker than specified, and exactly the gap PM asked CIO to verify (relayed via Docs, `relay-docs-to-cio-...-2026-09-22.md`). Fix: Step 5 now explicitly defines a "round" (one full 1-2-2b-3 pass), requires tracking empty-vs-non-empty per round, and exits only when the just-finished round AND the one before it were both empty. Verified this was a real gap, not a false alarm, before editing -- reported to PM directly per PM's own ask, shared with Janus per PM's cross-project framing.

v1.37 (2026-09-21) — **START's registry-row step now names the `state` column explicitly and requires clearing a stale `parked` value, not just implicitly assuming a full-row rewrite covers it.** Docs found their own row stayed `parked` through a clean day-close after the 09-20 Amber-reboot park event, because their STOP habit narratively rewrites `active_since` (col 7) but nothing in this skill's steps named col 8 (`state`) as something to check separately — CXO relayed the finding since it lands on this skill, not on Docs's own carry-forward. Same shape as finding #6 (a missing row is invisible to the watchdog) one column over: a row that EXISTS but lies about being parked is just as invisible, in the opposite direction — the watchdog treats `parked` as "deliberately dark, no stall alerts," so a role stuck reading `parked` while actually fine loses real future-stall coverage, silently. Fix: the START registry-row paragraph's column list now includes `state` explicitly, with its own instruction to clear a stale `parked:` value at START (never STOP, never a peer) per the row's own clearing-condition text.

v1.36 (2026-09-19) — **Mail Loop gains step 1c: the unboarded-PM-items scan.** Exec's proposal (`mailboxes/cio/inbox/proposal-exec-to-cio-...-2026-09-18.md`), built and tested against a real miss (HOST's alpha-tester invite memo triaged `inbox/`→`read/` in the same commit, landing on a surface no board sweep ever read, sat unsurfaced 5 days). CIO's ruling on the three open questions: (1) **scope split** — every role runs `scripts/check-unboarded-pm-items.sh {role} --scope=role --since-last-scan --record` every fire (own `read/` + own standing-items, cheap, role-scoped); only the board-compiling role additionally runs `--scope=global` (PM's inbox + commit bodies, identical for all 11 roles — no reason to print it 11x/cycle). Shipped as a `--scope` flag on the script itself (`scripts/check-unboarded-pm-items.sh`) rather than left as a convention nobody enforces. (2) **marker location** — moved from `dev/active/{role}-last-pm-scan` to `dev/state/{role}-last-pm-scan`: `dev/active/` is sprint-cleaned, so a marker there silently degrades the scan window to a 24h default the day it gets swept — coverage narrows with no error, the exact m-44 shape the script's own output already warns about elsewhere. `dev/state/` is a new, not-swept home for small durable machine-state markers (as opposed to `dev/active/`'s human-scratch/carry-forward role). (3) **hook vs. skill** — stays in `duty-cycle-tick`, not the SessionStart hook: SessionStart fires once per session, not once per cron tick, and this scan's whole value (hours-not-between-boards cadence) depends on running every fire. Hits are candidates, not verdicts — a clean run is not a claim that nothing needs PM (the script says so in its own footer; this skill doesn't repeat it as a verdict).

v1.35 (2026-09-12) — **Step 5b's self-check gains the denominator it was missing.** CXO adopted v1.34 same-day, found it works, and caught the exact numerator-without-denominator shape in it that CXO independently caught in their own scope-guard verdict-count proposal two days earlier: "no output for your role name" is itself ambiguous between genuinely clean and the script having died or changed format. Fix costs nothing -- the script already prints its own denominator ( in the header line); the step now reads the full output and confirms rows is non-zero before treating an unmatched grep as a measured absence.

v1.34 (2026-09-12) — **Step 5b gains a self-verification line: check duty-cycle-freeze-check.sh's own output for your own role name instead of trusting you ran the heartbeat command.** Exec found CIO (this skill's own author) went two consecutive days with zero heartbeat invocations despite 8+ real commits/day, caught both times by a colleague reading a cohort-attention rollup rather than by anything in this skill itself. Root cause: on a busy fire, running Step 5b and skipping it produce the identical local output (nothing) -- so there was no in-the-moment feedback that the step had been skipped, the exact mechanism-vs-vigilance failure m-36 names. Fix: grep the freeze-check's already-existing per-role output for your own name after Step 5b; BELT-INVISIBLE/NO-SESSION-LOG there is an in-fire action item, not something to wait for a colleague to notice.

v1.33 (2026-09-12) — **FOUR changes, all from one week's mail thread, bundled into one focused pass rather than four piecemeal edits (deliberately deferred to this fresh session, named trigger, per the spine's own quality-banking rule).** (1) **The work queue is now THREE sources for every role**, not build-capable-roles-only: PM's ruling generalizes v1.32's Sprint-Backlog-only patch into carried-work + mail + a per-role GitHub criteria line (HOST's Step 1a was the prototype, 8 months early). Folded in CXO's same-week finding that a criteria line's own wording must require `gh issue view` on each result, not just `gh issue list` — a list is a fragment, and CXO wrote 2 of 3 tracker rows wrong from list output alone before catching it. (2) **START's session-log commit now precedes the mail loop, not follows it** (CXO's structural fix) — closes the NO-SESSION-LOG race at its source (a `mail(role):` push landing before the log commit, which `duty-cycle- freeze-check.sh`'s grace window only mitigated, not removed) rather than only detecting it after the fact. (3) **`## Fire N` retired as the session-log heading default** — Exec found the skill says "fire" 58 times against 2 anti-chunking warnings, and while a same-morning 8-seat self-audit found the actual chunking behavior mostly isn't happening, the heading convention itself demonstrably costs Docs real synthesis effort reconstructing "what shipped" from "what happened at wake N" across 11 differently-formatted logs. New default: head by work unit, wake time as a parenthetical. (4) **Anomalous readings (freeze checks, BELT-INVISIBLE, NO-SESSION-LOG) get re-checked once before being reported** — PM's rule, from watching Exec publish a stall that had already resolved 4 minutes earlier. Composes with m-44 (a clear isn't a measurement) as its converse (an alert isn't one either, if it's one sample of a moving value). Exec's other two proposals from the same thread (archiving `mailboxes/*/read/`, changing the PM-cc rule) are deliberately NOT in this edit — they're mailbox/communication policy, not fire procedure, and belong in CLAUDE.md's mailbox section or a standalone archival tool, not this skill.

v1.32 (2026-09-08) — **TWO PM-ruled amendments, same morning, both CIO's to write.** (1) **Task Loop gains BACKLOG INTAKE**: PM found the structural defect in one sentence — the flywheel's work- definition is exactly two surfaces (mail + own standing-items), so "there is no work" and "28 open items" were both true at once, and Lead's quiet WATCH fires were the procedure executing correctly, not a failure to notice work. Exec's finding, PPM's eligibility denominator (open + MVP + Sprint Backlog, re-derivable from `sprint-truth.py`, no new artifact) and claim convention (`updateProjectV2ItemFieldValue`, first-write-wins — sufficient while Lead is the only consumer), Arch's denominator refinement (the drained-state line must enumerate what it checked, not just assert "nothing left," so the next missing surface announces itself instead of hiding behind an all-clear for months the way this one did). CIO's ruling on Exec's own question (edge case or exception to anti-instrument-sprawl?): **edge case, not exception** — this adds no new artifact, no new reminder, no new tracking surface; it corrects an incomplete DEFINITION inside an already- mandatory step (the Task Loop's own exit condition), which is exactly what the principle protects rather than what it forbids. Scoped to build-capable roles only (role-scoping itself is a separate, still-open question — PPM/Arch's Q1). (2) **START gains a carry-forward REFRESH + RE-VERIFY step**, PM-ruled cohort norm: refresh happens at START, not only end-of-fire, because the failure mode is the long quiet stretch (WATCH fires correctly change nothing, so an end-of-fire refresh never runs on the days that cause the problem) — AND rewriting is not the same as re-verifying (three roles' carry-forwards were all dated within a day, all regularly rewritten, and all still carried a resolved PM-gated item forward inside the rewrite). Three re-verification surfaces per PM-gated row (Exec's, from a 09-06 near-miss): decisions.log + purpose-built docs, the recipient's own `sent/`, GitHub state. **CIO's honest caveat, stated rather than hidden**: as shipped, the re-verify half is PROSE, not a chokepoint — nothing stops a rewrite without a real check, which is precisely this week's own self-attestation-is-not-verification finding (m-50) applied to the instrument meant to catch exactly that. Shipping the prose rule now per PM's ruling; naming the mechanization gap explicitly rather than pretending the prose closes it.

v1.31 (2026-08-30) — **Step 3 START gains an opt-in tracked-state currency check.** CXO's design (docs/internal/design/tracked-state-staleness-design-2026-08-29.md), CIO's `--state-files` mode (shipped same day, `cd85d4664`): a carry-forward's currency claim is CADENCE-shaped, checkable only against time, not an artifact — different predicate from the trigger-time class this skill's Mail Loop already covers. CXO measured all 11 real carry-forwards before designing this: 7 of 11 declared no date at all, and CXO's own header was actively wrong at the moment of measuring. Opt-in only (a role adds `currency_claim`/`max_age_days` frontmatter when it chooses; two roles — arch, cxo — had already adopted it independently before the checker shipped); nothing to report for a role that hasn't adopted it yet.

v1.30 (2026-08-28) — **Mail Loop drain gains an explicit sync-first rationale, PM-ratified as a standing rule.** CXO's finding: Lead's fire-opener ran `ls mailboxes/lead/inbox/` before `git merge origin/main`, so mail already on `origin/main` (CXO's own design position) was invisible for a full fire — Lead reported the thread quiet and asked PM to nudge CXO for something CXO had already sent. The skill's Step 2→3 ordering was already correct; it just never stated WHY, so a hand-written fire-opener could get it backwards without anyone noticing (`ls inbox/` reads as "check state first," which sounds harmless). Same shape as v1.26's Step 2c relocation (a component reading local state before a fetch reports on the previous fire's world). PM, in conversation: "a fresh sync with origin main should precede any mail check" — now a ratified rule, not a suggestion to weigh. Denominator note (CXO's own): only two fire-openers were actually checked (Lead's, defective; CXO's, correct) — whether the other nine order it correctly is unmeasured.

v1.29 (2026-08-15) — **Step 1 gains a proactive-expiry bullet: re-arm BEFORE the 7-day cap, not after discovering it's gone.** Lead's proposal, from their own Agent 360 finding (10.3/10.5): `CronCreate`'s 7-day auto-expiry is announced once at creation and nowhere else, and the existing Gap-C self-heal only fires reactively (after the job is already dead, on whatever turn the session next gets). Zero new mechanism — the skill already rotates crons on delete-then-create — this only moves the trigger from "noticed it's gone" to "noticed it's going," using each agent's own recorded arm-date (carry-forward / registry state column — several rows already carry this by hand) since `CronList` doesn't return job-creation timestamps.

v1.28 (2026-08-10) — **Step 1c's rate-caution refined: name the timescale, don't let readings at different scales silently override each other.** The prior wording ("don't extrapolate from one interval") wasn't quite the actual lesson — CIO's daily figure (3 lines/day, 24h) was right and stable; both CIO's own later retraction of it (using a 6h lull) and HOST's earlier "~22 hours" estimate (from a single interval) were wrong the same way: an intraday reading silently overrode or was mistaken for a daily rate. Fixed the wording to say what actually failed.

v1.27 (2026-08-09) — **Step 1c: fixed to read the generator's guard-convention line count instead of a bare `wc -l`.** CIO and HOST independently reported 15 vs. 14 headroom for the identical file the same day — a trailing-newline artifact makes plain `wc -l` under-count by 1 relative to `n_lines = body.count("\n") + 1`, the number `rebuild-memory-index.py`'s own truncation guard actually checks. Now reads `L` straight from `check-derived-drift.sh`'s already-run output instead of a separate shell-out, eliminating the two-convention ambiguity at the source rather than documenting it. Also added: don't extrapolate a headroom rate from one interval — CIO measured 0.25/h then 0.00/h across two consecutive windows on the same file, and both agents' single-interval ETAs turned out unsupportable.

v1.26 (2026-08-09) — **Step 1b (cohort-freeze check) RELOCATED from before Step 2's sync to just after it (now Step 2c).** Web's finding: the detector reads local `dev/heartbeats/*.tsv` and never fetches, so running it before a sync produces a false COHORT-FREEZE from a stale checkout, not a real cohort signal — for a multi-hour cadence, close to the common case. Web caught it before it became a false full-cohort-freeze alert to PM (verified via re-run post-fetch, rc=1→rc=0 same window, cross-checked against `git log origin/main`). Step 1a and 1c stay in place — neither has this staleness dependency (1a hits GitHub live, 1c reads a non-git local path). A stub remains at the old Step 1b location pointing to the new one, per this file's own don't-leave-a-silent-gap discipline.

v1.25 (2026-08-08) — **Step 1c added: cheap per-fire MEMORY.md headroom check, HOST-only.** Direct answer to Exec's ask (08-07 PM-DECISION memo) whether periodic consolidation belongs on the recurring-task surface. `wc -l` the shared index every fire; <15 lines headroom → note it; <8 → escalate by mail. Triggered by a live near-miss the same fire this shipped: `check-derived-drift.sh` caught MEMORY.md one entry stale (174→175) for the first time on real state rather than a reconstruction, and regenerating it landed at 195/200 lines — 5 lines from the documented silent-truncation ceiling. Does not prune or decide anything itself; the doc's own governance constraint (deletion is irreversible, shared pool, not a formatting call) means this step only watches and reports.

v1.24 (2026-08-08) — **Step 1b added: check for a cohort-wide freeze at wake time, all roles.** HOST's own half of the HOST↔CIO freeze-monitor seam (CIO's `cohort-freeze-detect.sh` shipped 08-07). At START/WATCH, run the detector; on COHORT-FREEZE, state the gap was environmental in the fire-open line instead of treating it as a personal stall, and skip self-diagnosing a gap the detector already explained at cohort scope — same "you cannot detect absence from a surface authored by the party whose absence is in question" principle (Arch/PPM, 08-06) applied to your own wake. Explicitly does NOT cover alerting PM *during* a freeze (every duty cycle is frozen then too — needs a watcher outside the frozen set, CIO/Pard's to build); HOST's stake there is the alert's content (state what was measured), not its delivery.

v1.23 (2026-08-01) — **★ READING A tmux PANE: a line in the input box is NOT a message. Read ABOVE the separator.** CIO added a *"check the pane before reporting a silence"* step after twice mistaking a conversing role for a stalled one — then used it to make the opposite and worse error. On 7/31 it read `lead`'s pane, saw `❯ ok keys are in the keychain now, try #1445 again`, and reported *"a live exchange with PM; the silence is the conversation, not a freeze."* **It was an unsent draft sitting in the composer.** PM confirmed never writing it. Janus captured the pane and named the tells: the `❯` prompt marker and the status bar *below* it, with the session's last real activity (`✻ Cogitated…`) *above* the separator. CIO then verified the pane was **byte-identical 30 hours later** — a live exchange would have moved. **Lead was genuinely stalled for two days**, its wake sat on Exec's critical path, and the false all-clear **propagated**: Janus relayed it to PM as fact and glossed an Exec memo with a claim it does not make. **Method, amended (Janus's, adopted verbatim): read ABOVE the input separator for an actually-submitted exchange, or corroborate with an artifact a real exchange produces — a commit, a log line, a mail. A quoted "user" line in the input box is a CLAIM ABOUT the user, not a message FROM them.** Cheap second check: **capture twice, minutes apart** — a frozen pane is identical, a live one moves. This is m-43 *inside a check written to prevent m-43*: right surface, wrong region of it.

v1.22 (2026-07-29) — **★ Step 2a-bis RETIRED. Stop probing; the bug is fixed at the mechanism.** Arch's architectural ruling: the defect was never shape, index state, timing or layer — it was a **time-of-check/time-of-use inversion.** `check-branch.sh` reads `git diff --cached`, and as a **PreToolUse** hook it runs *before* the command it gates. For `git add mailboxes/… && git commit …` it inspects the index that the very command it is gating is about to populate. Empty index → allow. Pard then installed a real `.git/hooks/pre-commit` in the **common dir**, covering every worktree by construction and *delegating* to `check-branch.sh` so the two can never fork; verified BLOCKED on the compound bypass class, ALLOWED on a non-mail control. **Two defects died with it**: the mute-block problem (git surfaces pre-commit output directly) and the need for index-state controls in any probe. **So the both-shape probe, the index assertion, and the whole probe apparatus this week built are retired — they were instrumentation for characterising one bug that no longer exists.** Do NOT run 2a-bis on a new seat; confirm the common-dir `pre-commit` hook exists instead. Keep the mitigation only as a habit, not a requirement: staging in one call and committing in a separate bare call was never the fix, just a shape that happened to dodge the inversion.

v1.21 (2026-07-28) — **HEARTBEAT: quiet fires must now emit one machine-readable liveness line.** The belt inferred liveness from WORK OUTPUT while this skill tells you not to produce any on a quiet fire — so a compliant quiet fire was invisible BY CONSTRUCTION and the watchdog **alerted on compliance** (lead flagged 3x on 07-27 while alive). No threshold fixes that; widening only trades false positives for latency, unacceptably so for low-frequency roles (exec would need 25h). **On every fire, run `scripts/duty-cycle-heartbeat.sh {role} {START|WATCH|WORK|STOP} --if-quiet`.** `--if-quiet` is the whole cost control: if the fire already produced a role-tagged commit, that commit IS the heartbeat and nothing is written — so busy agents pay ~zero and only the currently-invisible fires cost anything (HOST refinement a). Per-role daily files = rotation and zero write contention by construction (refinement b). A broken WRITER is distinguishable from a quiet cohort via `HEARTBEAT-WRITER-SILENT` in freeze-check

v0.8 (refinement c / G6). **This is NOT a logging surface** — the session log remains the single canonical record; what §5 forbids is a near-duplicate PROSE entry, which is why that same line requires one-line WATCH/START entries. HOST-approved 07-28.

v1.20 (2026-07-27) — **v1.17 has an unstated precondition: the agent must be RUNNING.** "Write your own registry row" is right for a LIVE role — only it knows its cron expression — but a **PARKED role has no armed cron, so it never wakes to read the ask.** CIO mailed `arch`/`cxo` to fix their own stale rows and HOST caught within hours that the request was structurally unsatisfiable by its recipients: the detector fired correctly for 3.5h, the mail sat in inboxes nobody would open. **The rule splits: live role with a bad row → the agent fixes it (v1.17 unchanged); parked role with a bad row → only a HUMAN or the registry owner can, because the only actor who could is switched off.** Same shape for a missing row on a parked role. Route those asks to a person, never to the role.

v1.19 (2026-07-26) — **Step 2a-bis must run BOTH command shapes.** PA/CXO/PPM, 14 probes, three fresh seats: standalone blocks 4/4, compound bypasses 7/10. The old single-probe wording reads as standalone, so it certified coverage agents did not have — a false pass *inside* the verification protocol. Both shapes now required and reported separately; free mitigation added (stage in one call, commit in a separate bare call = gated).

v1.18 (2026-07-25) — **URGENT correction to Step 2a-bis: a bare block is NOT the pass.** Lead Dev ran the probe within hours of v1.15 shipping and the **permission classifier intercepted the mailbox-commit before git hooks could run** — producing a refusal indistinguishable from a hook block. So the pass signal was producible by something other than the mechanism under test: a false-confidence path *inside the verification protocol itself*, which is the exact shape findings #4/#5/#6 all had. Pass now keys on check-branch.sh's **distinctive output** (`BLOCKED: You are on branch …`), which nothing else emits. Classifier refusal = **INCONCLUSIVE, gate stays closed**; do not work around the denial to force a probe through.

v1.17 (2026-07-25) — **START owns your watchdog registry row** (finding #6 fix). The freeze-watchdog registry is an OPT-IN list; no row = structurally invisible when you die, and the belt reports the cohort clear while you're dark (it covered 4/10 roles and phrased its subset as a total; five roles dark six days). The intuitive fix — provisioner writes the row at standup — **does not work**, because the row's load-bearing field is the cron expression and that isn't known until the agent arms it. Proven at HOST's provisioning the same day: Pard correctly declined to guess-edit and deferred, so agent #2 came up live and unwatched. So START now verifies-or-writes the row right after arming the cron. You always know your own cadence; nobody else does.

v1.16 (2026-07-25) — **collision guard SHIPPED**: Step 2a's tmux-cwd gate is no longer a forward-reference. Pard shipped and tested it in `amber-agent.sh` the same day — at worktree standup it refuses to launch if any existing tmux session's cwd is that worktree or a subdir (verified: a dummy session occupying a worktree blocked a second launch, nothing spawned). The Model-A branch-name false-pass is therefore **genuinely superseded**, not merely documented as unreliable. Changed "will be" to "is" so a cohort-wide procedure isn't carrying a dangling promise.

v1.15 (2026-07-25) — **Model-A collision re-derivation + hooks behavioral check** (CIO, Pard concurring on detection). Step 2's worktree model is now HOST-DEPENDENT (Model A on Amber, Model B on Desktop, PM-ratified 2026-07-25) — read "ephemeral worktree" as "your worktree"; sync/push mechanics are identical. **Step 2a rewritten**: the branch-name-contains-basename fingerprint is a Model-B artifact and on Model A it passes whether or not a collision exists (the pairing is permanent by construction), so keeping it as the only gate is *worse* than none — it returns a confident pass. The Model-A collision is two live sessions sharing one worktree cwd; the real gate is tmux-side in `amber-agent.sh` (Pard-owned), reflog is the forensic tell not the gate, and unexplained state defaults to your own pre-compaction work rather than a phantom peer. **Step 2a-bis added** (Finding #4, 2026-07-25): verify enforcement hooks actually FIRE on your first fire in a worktree — project hooks were found silently inactive with config present, correctly registered, and working when invoked by hand. Config presence proves nothing; a block on a probe `mailboxes/` commit is the pass, silence is a fail. Full protocol in `docs/internal/operations/amber-worktree-lifecycle.md` Rule 4.

v1.13 (2026-06-17) — **escalations-doc FOLD** (PM-ratified 2026-06-17): REMOVED the methodology-41 STOP-reconcile step + deprecated the per-role `duty-cycle-escalations-{role}.md` docs. The hand-maintained reconcile was itself vigilance-dependent and rotted anyway (Exec 6/17: most docs 1–3 weeks stale *despite* the step — the very pattern it was meant to prevent). Replaced by mechanisms: the cohort-attention rollup GitHub-verifies every item + the freeze-registry handles liveness; PM-attention/escalation items ride the **carry-forward** (residual home), genuine escalations go via **mail** (signaling layer). State-files table + Step-3 STOP updated.

v1.12 (2026-06-16) — **boundary SHARPENED** (PM 2026-06-16, correcting v1.11's over-permissiveness): the quality-banking exception is valid ONLY with an **explicit, real trigger** named out loud — a fresh session or a context compaction. "No rush" / "not urgent" / "deserves focus" with no named trigger is NOT a reason — it's the antipattern in a quality costume ("shyness should not be a thing; there is no advantage to saving work"). Two states only: do-it-now, or explicit-real-trigger-wait.

v1.11 (2026-06-16) — **boundary added** to the Core-model callout (log-sweep + prior-art finding): drain-all-unblocked EXCEPT deep / quality-sensitive work that genuinely warrants a fresh focused pass — that's *quality-banking*, not bite-sizing. The test is WHY you defer (to pace the cron / because a "fire" ended = antipattern; for genuine quality = fine). Resolves the both-ways ambiguity PM ruled on 6/15 (endorsed Lead's quality-banking the same day as nudging against pacing-deferral).

v1.10 (2026-06-16) — **DRAIN-UNTIL-EMPTY / fire-as-wake-not-time-box** (PM-surfaced 2026-06-15, HOST-named in the methodology memo): added the Core-model callout to the Procedure header (a cron fire is a WAKE, not a time-box → on waking, drain ALL unblocked work; a commit is git-hygiene, NOT a stop) + clarified in Step 5 that the "Fire N" label records which *wakeup* initiated the work, NOT a work-unit boundary. Fixes the cohort-wide bite-sizing antipattern (agents doing one task per fire then stopping while unblocked work remained).

v1.9 (2026-06-15) — **Pre-staging discipline** added to Step 6: `git reset HEAD` before staging clears rebase/autostash index residue; prevents wrong-files-in-commit failure (Web 2026-06-15 incident: autostash debris swept into session-log close-out commit).

v1.8 (2026-06-12) — **SINGLE-SURFACE logging** (PM-ratified 2026-06-12: "simplify logging, minimize drift — do the logging in one place"): the **session log** is now THE single canonical record (per-fire entry + day-wrap go there); the cycle log is demoted to **optional private scratch** (not a logging surface, not a parallel record). Supersedes the v1.5 dual-surface design — one place, not two; removes drift at the source rather than guarding against it. m-31 amendment + cohort broadcast queued (CIO).

v1.7 (2026-06-12) — Step-3 Mail Loop now ends with the #1106 MANIFEST derive: recipient regenerates own MANIFESTs via `scripts/regenerate-mailbox-manifests.py --role {role-slug}` (subject→H1→warned-(no subject) precedence; curated content below `<!-- curated -->` marker preserved verbatim; whole-state regen = idempotent, closes the write-contention race structurally — recipient is sole writer).

v1.6 (2026-06-12) — Step-3 STOP dispatch rule changed from "past ~11pm" to **"this is the last scheduled fire of today"** (compute next-fire-time from cron expression; if its calendar date differs from today's, STOP this fire). PM-coined rule. Fixes cohort-wide STOP-never-triggers gap for windowed shapes ending before 22:00 (Exec 6/12 finding; PA confirmed identical ad-hoc improvisation in flight). Works elegantly for every shape (continuous + all windowed variants).

v1.5 (2026-06-09) — Step-5 DUAL-SURFACE logging: substantive fires now accrete a one-line summary to the SESSION log (durable; what Docs reads for omnibus) in addition to the cycle log (ephemeral working state). Fixes the session-log-vs-cycle-log displacement PM flagged 2026-06-09 16:48 + Arch analyzed: the fire loop referenced only the cycle log, so the session log silently displaced → institutional-memory leak (cycle logs live in `dev/active/` and get cleaned at sprint boundaries; only the dated session log is durable). Mechanism-beats-vigilance (m-36): displacement becomes impossible-by-construction. Composes with m-31 (cycle log lives ALONGSIDE, not in place of, the session log).

v1.4 (2026-06-09) — START Step-0 self-heal: verify the prior day STOPped (grep the `<!-- DAY-CLOSED: {date} -->` marker) + run its missed close if not (PM-ratified, Comms-surfaced — fixes the day-ends-without-STOP → session-log-never-closes gap); STOP now emits the canonical DAY-CLOSED marker.

v1.3 (2026-06-07) — Step-1 Gap-C self-heal: re-arm if CronList shows zero crons (compaction can silently kill a session cron; durable=noop). Partial mitigation (heals on next turn); Routines watchdog is the cure.

v1.2 (2026-06-07) — Step-3 overnight-window guard: state+hour hybrid so the continuous shape's ~2am WATCH doesn't mis-START (caught by CIO dogfood overnight 6/6→7); overnight branch checked first + hour-gated.

v1.1 (2026-06-06) — Step-3 routes by STATE not clock-hour (HOST finding) for low-freq/Web shapes; Rule-2 keep-armed-default (PM). v1.0 — initial (CIO, gbrain thin-job-prompt adoption).

